Software development is becoming a continuous stream of changes. An agent edits
code, runs checks, revises, and tries again—often while other agents work on the
same repository. Most teams know the CI loop that follows: push a change, wait
for a cloud runner to install the project, run the checks, and report a status
on the pull request. The cloud runner gives everyone a shared answer. It is
slower and more expensive than a warm checkout on a developer's machine, but it
gives the team a place to trust.

For an agent, the local loop is the work: edit, check, revise, repeat. The agent
already has the source, dependencies, and tools on disk, so it can get useful
feedback locally in seconds. Sending every attempt back to a fresh cloud runner
adds setup time and another CI bill.

That does not mean a local green terminal should replace CI. The local machine
may have run the wrong command, reported only the checks that passed, or tested
a checkout that is no longer the code that will merge. The question is whether
the work already done locally can help with the merge decision without asking
GitHub to trust an unexplained report.

The opportunity is to let local work travel with the code as evidence, so remote
CI does not need to rediscover everything from scratch.

{% mermaid label="Local checks produce a proof that the Thwip App can verify" %}
```mermaid
flowchart LR
  A["Agent edits a pull request"] --> B["Local machine runs<br/>required checks"]
  B --> C["Sign proof for<br/>exact Git tree"]
  C --> D["Thwip App verifies<br/>the signed proof"]
  D --> E["Merge decision"]
```
{% /mermaid %}

Evidence only helps if it travels with the right code. There is a second
problem even when the local run was completely honest. CI on a pull request
usually checks the pull request's head. The code that lands may also include
newer changes from `main`. If those two snapshots differ, a green result for the
first one does not say anything certain about the second. [The merge-queue
post](/blog/merge-queue/) explains how thwip builds and pins that integrated
candidate.

That is the bridge thwip is trying to build: local work produces a clear, signed
claim about exactly what it checked, and the Thwip App compares that claim with
the code under review. If the team wants a second execution, GitHub Actions can
still run the checks itself. The code and its evidence can move together, while
the policy decides how much of that evidence is enough.

## What the proof says

A proof is a signed statement about a concrete result. In plain language, it
says something like:

> These required checks passed on this exact snapshot of the repository, under
> this project's rules, using this check recipe, and this device key signed the
> statement.

The exact snapshot matters. Git calls its snapshot of file names, file contents,
and file permissions a [tree](https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Internals-Git-Objects).
Git gives that tree a digest—a compact fingerprint of its contents. Change a
file, add a file, or change its permissions and the digest changes. A proof
that names the digest is therefore tied to the files that were checked, not
just to a branch name or a commit message.

thwip carries the proof on the commit in a `thwip-proof: v1...` Git trailer. The
trailer contains a signed statement, not a human-readable log. It uses two
standard building blocks:

- [in-toto](https://in-toto.io/) is a format for describing what happened to a
  software artifact as it moved through a process. thwip uses the Git tree as
  the artifact and records the checks that were claimed for it.
- [DSSE](https://github.com/secure-systems-lab/dsse) is a standard signed
  envelope. It keeps the statement's exact bytes together with the signature
  and the identifier of the key that signed them. If somebody edits the claim,
  signature verification fails.

To verify the claim, the App needs more than a tree digest and a pass/fail word.
The proof names the tree and the base it was built from, so the App can tell
which code and which version of `main` were involved. It names the policy, which
says what checks were required. It names the pipeline by a cryptographic digest
of the instructions for running those checks, similar to pinning a GitHub
Actions workflow definition. It also records the runner and timing, the
individual suite results, and the signing key's identity.

While a proof cannot remove every trust assumption, it lets the App compare the
claim with the change, the active policy, and the required checks instead of
treating local output as authoritative.

{% mermaid label="How local checks become a GitHub merge check" %}
```mermaid
flowchart TD
  A["Local machine runs<br/>required checks"] --> B["Proof names<br/>tree and results"]
  B --> C["Device key signs<br/>the proof"]
  C --> D["Thwip App verifies<br/>the signed proof"]
  D --> E{"Tree, signer, policy,<br/>and results match?"}
  E -- "no" --> F["Required check fails"]
  E -- "yes" --> G["Required check passes"]
  G --> H["Queue may merge"]
```
{% /mermaid %}

## How the GitHub App verifies it

The thwip GitHub App does not run repository code to make this decision. It
reads the commit and GitHub's view of the pull request, verifies the DSSE
signature using the enrolled signing key, and checks that the key belongs to a
GitHub user with enough repository permission.

It then compares the claim with the change under review: the tree digest, the
base, the active policy, the check recipe, and the required suite results. A
proof for one tree cannot satisfy a check for another. If the pull request head
has changed since the proof was made, the mismatch is visible and the App's
proof check fails. Once enforcement is active, that required check is `thwip`.
During onboarding, the App instead publishes `thwip / proof (observe)`, while
the setup workflow publishes the distinct `thwip / workflow` check. The queue's
moving authorization is `thwip / turn`; none of those publishers share a name.

The App does not need `Administration: write`. Repository administrators apply
the rulesets it renders. It does need `Workflows: write`, because GitHub will not
let the sole App writer land a reviewed change under `.github/workflows`
without that permission. That permission lets it write CI definitions; the
control plane still does not execute repository code.

When a queue asks a device to check an integrated candidate, it first gives the
device the candidate tree and required checks. thwip accepts the device's result
only when it names that same tree.

The App can verify who signed the bytes and what those bytes claim. It can
verify that the claim names the code and checks the expected policy. It cannot,
from the signature alone, independently observe every command or environment
condition behind a local run.

## What the signature does (and does not) establish

To see what the signature adds, start with hashing. A cryptographic hash turns
the bytes of a statement into a fixed-size digest. Change the statement and the
digest changes. A device signs that statement with a private key; the App uses
the matching public key to check the signature. That check tells the App that
the unchanged bytes were signed by the enrolled key associated with that
identity.

Inside those signed bytes is an assertion: named checks passed on a named Git
tree under a named policy and pipeline. The App can verify that assertion before
it allows the required check to pass.

The signature does not prove that the checks really ran or that the local
machine was uncompromised. A compromised device can still sign a false claim.

The signature gives GitHub a precise, tamper-evident claim to evaluate. A second
GitHub run provides a different kind of evidence, at a different cost.

## Four ways to pay for confidence

Policies choose how much local work to carry forward and how much to repeat
remotely. Different projects will make different tradeoffs about where to place
trust and where to spend compute. thwip puts those choices in four profiles:

| Profile | What supplies the evidence | Trade-off |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `fast` | Reuse a signed proof on the pull request head. | Lowest cost and latency; the integrated candidate is not run again. |
| `fresh` | A device runs the queue's exact integrated candidate and signs a receipt. | No cloud rerun; the project trusts and needs that runner. |
| `remote` | A pinned GitHub Actions verifier runs the candidate. | GitHub supplies the second party; each candidate costs CI time. |
| `audited` | Run on a device, then rerun the required checks in GitHub Actions. | Strongest evidence, with both execution costs. |

At the policy level, the profiles decide where confidence comes from. `fast`
places the trust in the enrolled key and the machine that produced the proof.
`fresh` adds a signed result from a device that ran the exact tree required for
the integration. `remote` places execution with GitHub. `audited` combines a
device-run proof with a GitHub rerun. The policy therefore makes the trust
choice explicit without changing what a tree-bound proof means.

The cost difference is measurable. The local-evidence model is about keeping the
fast agent loop while avoiding unnecessary repeated setup. Compare the time a
warm local checkout spends running the suites with the time a cloud job spends
before it can start: runner setup, checkout, dependency installation, and build.
For an agent running many iterations, that setup repeats at every remote run. A
local proof reuses work already done; a remote verifier pays the setup to gain an
independent execution.

GitHub also bills runner time by runner class and rounds jobs up to whole
minutes. [Its published rates](https://docs.github.com/en/billing/reference/actions-minute-multipliers)
show the cloud cost. `fresh` avoids that cloud rerun but asks the project to
trust a device. `remote` pays for a shared execution environment. `audited`
pays for both. thwip makes that choice explicit instead of hiding it inside a
green check.

## The remaining tradeoffs

Proofs complement code review. A passing suite says that named checks passed on
a named tree. It does not establish that the product is secure or that the
tests cover the important behavior.

As with any technology choice, the tradeoff is where trust and cost land. Under
`fast`, there is no second execution, so a compromised signing device could
produce a false first proof. `fresh` requires a signed result from a device run;
`remote` and `audited` add GitHub execution. A suite that depends on a cloud
service, a platform-specific runner, or a secret may still belong in remote CI.

That is the larger shift: agents can do more than produce code and ask cloud CI
to start from scratch. They can carry evidence tied to the exact tree they
worked on into the systems that review and merge it. As agentic development
becomes more continuous and parallel, the ability to move verifiable evidence
alongside the code may become as important as the code itself.

